# OOP Concepts question bank (language-independent ideas, examples in C++ and Java)
@subject OOP Concepts | OOP | #DB2777 | Classes, encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, object lifetime and design principles

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Classes & Objects

[EASY]
Q: A class is best described as:
A) An instance of an object
B) A blueprint that defines data and behaviour for objects
C) A function that returns a value
D) A variable stored in memory
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: An object is:
A) A blueprint
B) An instance of a class
C) A keyword
D) A data type only for numbers
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which of these is NOT one of the four pillars of OOP?
A) Encapsulation
B) Inheritance
C) Compilation
D) Polymorphism
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: The four pillars are encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism.

[EASY]
Q: The variables declared inside a class (not inside a method) are called:
A) Local variables
B) Data members / fields / attributes
C) Parameters
D) Arguments
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: In C++, members of a class are by default:
A) public
B) private
C) protected
D) static
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: In a C++ struct the default is public; in a class it is private.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Box {
public:
    int w;
    Box(int w) : w(w) {}
};

int main() {
    Box a(5);
    Box b = a;
    b.w = 9;
    Box* p = &a;
    p->w += 1;
    cout << a.w << " " << b.w << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 6 9
B) 9 9
C) 5 9
D) 6 6
ANSWER: A
HINT: In C++, Box b = a makes a COPY; a pointer refers to the original object.
EXPLAIN: b is an independent copy, so changing b does not affect a. p points to a, so a.w becomes 6.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
class Box {
    int w;
    Box(int w) { this.w = w; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Box a = new Box(5);
        Box b = a;
        b.w = 9;
        System.out.println(a.w + " " + (a == b));
    }
}
END
A) 9 true
B) 5 false
C) 9 false
D) 5 true
ANSWER: A
HINT: In Java, object variables hold references. Compare with how C++ copies objects.
EXPLAIN: b = a copies the reference, so both names refer to one object; the change through b is visible through a.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
    int x, y;
public:
    Point(int x = 0, int y = 0) : x(x), y(y) {}
    int sum() const { return x + y; }
};

int main() {
    Point p1, p2(3), p3(3, 4);
    cout << p1.sum() << " " << p2.sum() << " " << p3.sum() << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 0 3 7
B) 0 0 7
C) Compilation error
D) 0 6 7
ANSWER: A
HINT: Default arguments fill in any missing parameters from the right.
EXPLAIN: p1 is (0,0), p2 is (3,0), p3 is (3,4), giving sums 0, 3 and 7.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Counter {
public:
    static int alive;
    Counter() { alive++; }
    ~Counter() { alive--; }
};
int Counter::alive = 0;

int main() {
    Counter a;
    {
        Counter b, c;
        cout << Counter::alive << " ";
    }
    Counter* d = new Counter();
    cout << Counter::alive << " ";
    delete d;
    cout << Counter::alive << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 3 2 1
B) 3 4 3
C) 3 2 2
D) 1 2 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: Objects declared inside { } are destroyed when the block ends; heap objects only when deleted.
EXPLAIN: a, b, c alive = 3; after the block b and c are destroyed (1), d is created (2); delete d leaves 1.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A {
public:
    int v;
    A(int v) : v(v) {}
    A(const A& o) : v(o.v * 10) { cout << "copy "; }
};

void byValue(A x) { cout << x.v << " "; }
void byRef(A& x) { cout << x.v << " "; }

int main() {
    A a(2);
    byRef(a);
    byValue(a);
    cout << a.v << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 2 copy 20 2
B) 2 copy 2 2
C) 20 copy 20 2
D) 2 20 copy 2
ANSWER: A
HINT: Passing by value invokes the copy constructor; passing by reference does not.
EXPLAIN: byRef prints 2 with no copy. byValue copies a (printing "copy" and setting v = 20) and prints 20. a itself is unchanged.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Encapsulation & Abstraction

[EASY]
Q: Encapsulation means:
A) Wrapping data and the methods that operate on it into a single unit and restricting direct access
B) Creating many objects
C) Inheriting from multiple classes
D) Converting code to machine code
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Abstraction means:
A) Showing only essential features and hiding implementation details
B) Copying objects
C) Running code faster
D) Using only private variables
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Getter and setter methods are mainly used to support:
A) Inheritance
B) Encapsulation
C) Recursion
D) Multithreading
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: In Java, which keyword declares a class that cannot be instantiated and may contain unimplemented methods?
A) final
B) abstract
C) static
D) private
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: In C++, a class becomes abstract when it has at least one:
A) Private member
B) Pure virtual function (= 0)
C) Static member
D) Constructor
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
class Account {
    private double balance;
    public boolean withdraw(double amt) {
        if (amt <= 0 || amt > balance) return false;
        balance -= amt;
        return true;
    }
    public void deposit(double amt) { if (amt > 0) balance += amt; }
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account a = new Account();
        a.deposit(500);
        a.deposit(-100);
        boolean ok1 = a.withdraw(700);
        boolean ok2 = a.withdraw(200);
        System.out.println(ok1 + " " + ok2 + " " + a.getBalance());
    }
}
END
A) false true 300.0
B) true true -400.0
C) false true 200.0
D) false false 500.0
ANSWER: A
HINT: The class validates every change before touching the private field.
EXPLAIN: The negative deposit is ignored, the 700 withdrawal is refused, the 200 withdrawal succeeds: 500 - 200 = 300.0.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which statement about a Java interface (Java 8+) and an abstract class is correct?
A) A class can implement many interfaces but extend only one abstract class
B) An abstract class cannot have constructors
C) Interfaces can have instance fields that change per object
D) A class can extend many abstract classes
ANSWER: A
HINT: Think about which of them would bring the multiple-inheritance diamond problem with state.
EXPLAIN: Interfaces carry no instance state, so multiple implementation is safe; abstract classes can have constructors and state but only single inheritance.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What happens when this Java code is compiled?
CODE java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape s = new Shape();
        System.out.println(s.area());
    }
}
END
A) Compilation error: Shape is abstract; cannot be instantiated
B) It prints 0.0
C) It throws a runtime exception
D) It prints null
ANSWER: A
HINT: Can you create an object of an abstract class directly?
EXPLAIN: An abstract class may only be instantiated through a concrete subclass (or an anonymous class that implements area()).

[HARD]
Q: Why is returning a direct reference to a private mutable list from a getter a problem?
CODE java
class Course {
    private final List<String> students = new ArrayList<>();
    public List<String> getStudents() { return students; }
}
END
A) Callers can modify the internal list (e.g. clear it), breaking encapsulation; return an unmodifiable view or a copy instead
B) It causes a compilation error because the field is final
C) Lists cannot be returned from methods
D) It is fine because the field is private
ANSWER: A
HINT: final only stops the field from pointing to a different list.
EXPLAIN: The caller gets the same list object and can call add/clear on it. Use Collections.unmodifiableList(students) or new ArrayList<>(students).

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() const = 0;
    virtual ~Shape() {}
};
class Sq : public Shape {
    double s;
public:
    Sq(double s) : s(s) {}
    double area() const override { return s * s; }
};
class Rect : public Shape {
    double w, h;
public:
    Rect(double w, double h) : w(w), h(h) {}
    double area() const override { return w * h; }
};

int main() {
    Shape* shapes[] = { new Sq(3), new Rect(2, 5), new Sq(1) };
    double total = 0;
    for (Shape* s : shapes) { total += s->area(); delete s; }
    cout << total << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 20
B) 19
C) 10
D) Compilation error
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each call to area() goes to the actual object's override through the virtual table.
EXPLAIN: 9 + 10 + 1 = 20. Shape cannot be instantiated, but pointers to it can hold concrete objects.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Inheritance

[EASY]
Q: Inheritance represents which kind of relationship?
A) has-a
B) is-a
C) uses-a
D) part-of
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Composition represents has-a.

[EASY]
Q: The class that is inherited FROM is called the:
A) Derived class
B) Base (parent / super) class
C) Child class
D) Friend class
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which type of inheritance is NOT supported for classes in Java but is supported in C++?
A) Single
B) Multilevel
C) Multiple
D) Hierarchical
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: In Java, which keyword calls the parent class constructor?
A) this()
B) super()
C) parent()
D) base()
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: A protected member is accessible in:
A) Only the same class
B) The same class and its subclasses (and in Java, the same package)
C) Any class anywhere
D) No class
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A {
public:
    A() { cout << "A+ "; }
    ~A() { cout << "A- "; }
};
class B : public A {
public:
    B() { cout << "B+ "; }
    ~B() { cout << "B- "; }
};

int main() {
    B b;
    cout << "| ";
    return 0;
}
END
A) A+ B+ | B- A-
B) B+ A+ | A- B-
C) A+ B+ | A- B-
D) B+ A+ | B- A-
ANSWER: A
HINT: Construction goes base -> derived; destruction is the reverse.
EXPLAIN: The base part is built first and destroyed last.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
class Vehicle {
    int wheels = 4;
    String info() { return "Vehicle " + wheels; }
}
class Bike extends Vehicle {
    int wheels = 2;
    String info() { return "Bike " + wheels + " " + super.wheels; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Bike();
        System.out.println(v.wheels + " " + v.info());
    }
}
END
A) 4 Bike 2 4
B) 2 Bike 2 4
C) 4 Vehicle 4
D) 2 Bike 2 2
ANSWER: A
HINT: Methods are overridden (chosen at runtime) but fields are only hidden (chosen by the reference type).
EXPLAIN: v.wheels uses the Vehicle field (4); info() runs Bike's version, which prints its own wheels (2) and super.wheels (4).

[MEDIUM]
Q: The "diamond problem" in multiple inheritance refers to:
A) Ambiguity when a class inherits the same member from two parents that share a common base
B) A class with four constructors
C) Circular references between objects
D) Inheriting from a final class
ANSWER: A
HINT: Draw D inheriting from B and C, both of which inherit from A.
EXPLAIN: D gets two copies of A's members; C++ solves it with virtual inheritance, Java avoids it by disallowing multiple class inheritance.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A { public: int x = 1; };
class B : virtual public A {};
class C : virtual public A {};
class D : public B, public C {};

int main() {
    D d;
    d.B::x = 5;
    d.C::x += 2;
    cout << d.x << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 7
B) 5
C) 3
D) Compilation error: ambiguous member
ANSWER: A
HINT: With virtual inheritance there is only ONE shared A sub-object inside D.
EXPLAIN: B::x and C::x refer to the same variable: set to 5, then 5 + 2 = 7. Without virtual, d.x would be ambiguous.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    ~Base() { cout << "~Base "; }
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() { cout << "~Derived "; }
};

int main() {
    Base* p = new Derived();
    delete p;
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) ~Base
B) ~Derived ~Base
C) ~Base ~Derived
D) Compilation error
ANSWER: A
HINT: The destructor is not virtual, so which destructor does delete p call?
EXPLAIN: Without a virtual destructor only ~Base runs (technically undefined behaviour), so Derived's resources leak. Base classes used polymorphically need virtual destructors.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Polymorphism

[EASY]
Q: Polymorphism means:
A) One interface, many forms (the same call behaves differently for different objects)
B) Hiding data
C) Creating one object
D) Using many variables
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Method overloading is an example of:
A) Compile-time (static) polymorphism
B) Run-time (dynamic) polymorphism
C) Encapsulation
D) Inheritance only
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Method overriding is resolved at:
A) Compile time
B) Run time
C) Link time
D) Load time
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: In C++, which keyword enables run-time polymorphism for a member function?
A) static
B) virtual
C) inline
D) friend
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Two methods with the same name but different parameter lists in the same class are:
A) Overridden
B) Overloaded
C) Hidden
D) Abstract
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    static String f(int x) { return "int"; }
    static String f(long x) { return "long"; }
    static String f(double x) { return "double"; }
    static String f(Object x) { return "Object"; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte b = 1;
        System.out.println(f(b) + " " + f(5L) + " " + f(2.0f) + " " + f("s"));
    }
}
END
A) int long double Object
B) Object long double Object
C) int int double Object
D) Compilation error: ambiguous call
ANSWER: A
HINT: The compiler picks the most specific overload, preferring widening primitive conversions over boxing.
EXPLAIN: byte widens to int; 5L is long; float widens to double; a String matches Object.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal {
public:
    void eat() { cout << "Animal-eat "; }
    virtual void speak() { cout << "Animal-speak "; }
};
class Cat : public Animal {
public:
    void eat() { cout << "Cat-eat "; }
    void speak() override { cout << "Meow "; }
};

int main() {
    Animal* a = new Cat();
    a->eat();
    a->speak();
    delete a;
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) Animal-eat Meow
B) Cat-eat Meow
C) Animal-eat Animal-speak
D) Cat-eat Animal-speak
ANSWER: A
HINT: Only virtual functions are dispatched by the object's actual type.
EXPLAIN: eat() is not virtual, so the pointer type (Animal) decides; speak() is virtual, so Cat's version runs.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Money {
public:
    int rupees;
    Money(int r) : rupees(r) {}
    Money operator+(const Money& o) const { return Money(rupees + o.rupees); }
    bool operator<(const Money& o) const { return rupees < o.rupees; }
};

int main() {
    Money a(150), b(250);
    Money c = a + b;
    cout << c.rupees << " " << (a < b) << endl;
    return 0;
}
END
A) 400 1
B) 400 true
C) 150250 1
D) Compilation error
ANSWER: A
HINT: Operator overloading lets + and < call the member functions. cout prints a bool as 1 or 0 by default.
EXPLAIN: a + b gives 400, and a < b is true, which cout prints as 1 unless std::boolalpha is set.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C++ program?
CODE cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    virtual void show(int x = 10) { cout << "Base " << x << endl; }
    virtual ~Base() {}
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void show(int x = 20) override { cout << "Derived " << x << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Base* p = new Derived();
    p->show();
    delete p;
    return 0;
}
END
A) Derived 10
B) Derived 20
C) Base 10
D) Base 20
ANSWER: A
HINT: The function body is chosen at runtime, but default arguments are filled in at compile time.
EXPLAIN: Dynamic dispatch runs Derived::show, but the default argument comes from the static type (Base*), which is 10.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
class A {
    void m(Object o) { System.out.print("A.Object "); }
}
class B extends A {
    void m(Object o) { System.out.print("B.Object "); }
    void m(String s) { System.out.print("B.String "); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A ref = new B();
        ref.m("hi");
        ((B) ref).m("hi");
    }
}
END
A) B.Object B.String
B) B.String B.String
C) A.Object B.String
D) B.Object B.Object
ANSWER: A
HINT: The compiler chooses the overload from the REFERENCE type's methods; the JVM then picks the override from the object's class.
EXPLAIN: Through an A reference only m(Object) is visible, which B overrides -> "B.Object". Through a B reference, m(String) is the most specific overload.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Design Principles & Patterns

[EASY]
Q: Which SOLID principle says a class should have only one reason to change?
A) Open/Closed Principle
B) Single Responsibility Principle
C) Liskov Substitution Principle
D) Interface Segregation Principle
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which design pattern ensures that a class has only one instance?
A) Factory
B) Observer
C) Singleton
D) Adapter
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: Composition represents which relationship?
A) is-a
B) has-a
C) depends-on only
D) inherits-from
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: The Open/Closed Principle says that software entities should be:
A) Open for modification, closed for extension
B) Open for extension, closed for modification
C) Always public
D) Never inherited
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which pattern notifies many dependent objects automatically when one object's state changes (e.g. GUI event listeners)?
A) Singleton
B) Observer
C) Builder
D) Prototype
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: Square extends Rectangle, but setting the width of a Square also changes its height, so code that works for Rectangles breaks for Squares. Which principle is violated?
A) Liskov Substitution Principle
B) Single Responsibility Principle
C) Dependency Inversion Principle
D) Don't Repeat Yourself
ANSWER: A
HINT: Can a Square be used everywhere a Rectangle is expected without surprising the caller?
EXPLAIN: LSP requires that subtypes be substitutable for their base types without breaking expected behaviour.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
interface Shape { double area(); }
class Circle implements Shape {
    double r; Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    public double area() { return 3 * r * r; }
}
class Square implements Shape {
    double s; Square(double s) { this.s = s; }
    public double area() { return s * s; }
}
class ShapeFactory {
    static Shape create(String type, double size) {
        switch (type) {
            case "circle": return new Circle(size);
            case "square": return new Square(size);
            default: throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
        }
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape a = ShapeFactory.create("circle", 2);
        Shape b = ShapeFactory.create("square", 3);
        System.out.println(a.area() + b.area());
    }
}
END
A) 21.0
B) 12.0
C) 9.0
D) 21
ANSWER: A
HINT: The factory hides which concrete class is created. Both areas are doubles.
EXPLAIN: Circle area = 3 x 2 x 2 = 12.0, Square area = 9.0, total 21.0 (printed as a double).

[MEDIUM]
Q: A high-level ReportService creates a MySQLDatabase object directly with new. Which principle suggests depending on a Database interface instead?
A) Dependency Inversion Principle
B) Interface Segregation Principle
C) Liskov Substitution Principle
D) Law of Demeter
ANSWER: A
HINT: High-level modules should not depend on low-level details.
EXPLAIN: DIP: both should depend on abstractions, so the database can be swapped (this project's DAO interfaces do exactly that).

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this Java program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

interface Listener { void onEvent(String e); }

class EventBus {
    private final List<Listener> listeners = new ArrayList<>();
    void subscribe(Listener l) { listeners.add(l); }
    void publish(String e) { for (Listener l : listeners) l.onEvent(e); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EventBus bus = new EventBus();
        StringBuilder log = new StringBuilder();
        bus.subscribe(e -> log.append("A:").append(e).append(" "));
        bus.subscribe(e -> log.append("B:").append(e.length()).append(" "));
        bus.publish("hi");
        bus.publish("bye");
        System.out.println(log.toString().trim());
    }
}
END
A) A:hi B:2 A:bye B:3
B) A:hi A:bye B:2 B:3
C) B:2 A:hi B:3 A:bye
D) A:hi B:hi A:bye B:bye
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each publish call notifies every subscriber in the order they subscribed.
EXPLAIN: This is the Observer pattern: for each event, A logs the text and B logs its length.

[HARD]
Q: Why is this lazy Singleton NOT thread-safe, and what is a simple fix?
CODE java
class Config {
    private static Config instance;
    private Config() {}
    public static Config get() {
        if (instance == null) instance = new Config();
        return instance;
    }
}
END
A) Two threads can both see null and create two instances; use an eager static final field or the holder-class idiom
B) The private constructor stops any instance from being created
C) Static methods cannot return objects
D) It is thread-safe because the field is static
ANSWER: A
HINT: Imagine two threads running the if check at the same time.
EXPLAIN: Check-then-act is a race. The initialisation-on-demand holder idiom (a nested static class) or a synchronized/volatile double-check fixes it.
